## Focus
Group Policy. Account policies at the domain level and a machine
inactivity lock scoped to workstations.

## What I did

### Password and account lockout policy
Edited the Default Domain Policy under Computer Configuration >
Policies > Windows Settings > Security Settings > Account Policies.

Password policy:
- Minimum length set to 7
- Complexity requirement enabled
- Maximum password age 90 days

Account lockout policy:
- Lockout threshold 5 invalid attempts
- Accepted the suggested 30 minute lockout duration and reset counter

Note on the length: 7 is a lab convenience choice so I am not typing a
long password every time I log in. The production baseline is 14 or
higher, which is what CIS and DoD guidance call for. This is a
deliberate exception, not the recommended value.

### Lab OU
Created Corp > Computers > Lab and moved my practice VM into it. This
OU has no screen lock link, so I can work on that box without the
policy kicking in. In a real environment the exemption would run the
other way. Admin machines and accounts get stricter policy, not looser,
since they are the higher value targets.

### Screen lock GPO
Created a new GPO named Workstation Screen Lock and linked it to
Corp > Computers > Workstations.

Setting: Computer Configuration > Policies > Windows Settings >
Security Settings > Local Policies > Security Options > Interactive
logon: Machine inactivity limit.

Set to 900 seconds. I originally set 120 to watch it trigger, then
raised it to 15 minutes rather than sit and wait.

Ran gpupdate /force on CLIENT01 and confirmed with
gpresult /r /scope computer that the GPO applied. I did not sit and
watch the lock actually fire, so this is confirmed applied but not
confirmed triggered.

## What I learned

The thing that came up over and over tonight was the split between
computer policy and user policy, and how it drives where a GPO gets
linked.

Password policy for domain accounts is a computer setting, not a user
setting. Domain controllers enforce it because they hold the account
database, so the policy has to reach the DC computer object. The DCs
live in the Domain Controllers OU off the domain root, which is why a
domain level link is the one that works. Linking the same policy to a
Users OU does nothing. Linking it to a Workstations OU does apply, but
only to the local SAM accounts on those machines, not to domain
accounts.

That last part corrected something I had wrong. I assumed workstations
do not have passwords because users do. Every Windows machine has its
own local account database. Being domain joined just means it usually
is not the one getting used.

Screen lock is the opposite scoping decision. It is a per machine
control, so it belongs on the OU that holds the machines. The policy is
evaluated before anyone logs in, which is also why you cannot exempt a
person from it. You can only exempt a computer.

A domain gets one password policy by default. Fine Grained Password
Policies are the way to give different rules to different accounts.
They apply to global security groups rather than OUs and are managed in
AD Administrative Center instead of Group Policy.

Also learned there are two ways to exempt a machine from a GPO. A
separate OU with no link, which is what I did, or security filtering
with an explicit Deny on Apply Group Policy in the Delegation tab. Deny
wins over Allow. The separate OU is easier to see later in GPMC.

